**Read:** [Modeling Metamorphic Rocks Using Equilibrium Thermodynamics — Lanari and Duesterhoeft](../sources/Metamorphic-Modeling-Lanari-Duesterhoeft.lingua#r_08B2X0N88PTC51CSQPZAGWAQV8), the complete effective-composition and equilibrium-domain discussion with primary references; [O uso de pseudosseções em Petrologia Metamórfica — Gengo and colleagues](../sources/Pseudossecoes-Gengo-Santos-Moraes-Szabo.lingua#r_00V3DDNE7B5SQHWP5NKFT0YJZR), the full composition/system preparation; [Electron-excited X-ray microanalysis by EDS at 50 — Newbury and Ritchie](../sources/NIST-Quantitative-EDS.lingua#r_7XJDCAH01NX65F6Q5YY3CBFTCF), the full relevant quantitative-map and composition treatment.

A bulk-rock analysis samples one material support; an effective reacting composition describes the components available to the modeled domain/event. They can differ because of inherited cores, sequestration in growing phases, melt/fluid loss, reaction fronts, transport, or incomplete equilibration.

Choose a domain through mapped textures, composition, spatial relationships, and process evidence. Removing a grain fraction from a spreadsheet needs a material/chemical justification and a consistent balance. The remaining composition may depend on assumed density, mode, zoning, or component transfer.

Uncertainty in effective composition can shift phase fields and inferred conditions more than numerical refinement. Selecting a composition only because it makes a desired assemblage fit risks circular inference. Compare independent observations and plausible domain histories instead of tuning unmeasured components without exposing the choice.

**Work:** reconstruct a published effective-composition calculation from full methods, modes/maps, and mineral chemistry. Independently close a component balance and compare a whole-rock, core-excluded, or fluid-modified alternative as appropriate. State which data support each and how the choice changes the inferred assemblage or conditions.

Read the entire domain/model review and Portuguese calculation principles. The following pseudosection should display not just a preferred diagram but the sensitivity of conclusions to geologically plausible changes in the reacting system.

